  "calendar": "julian",
  "register": "documentary, and skeptical of Marco Polo's round numbers",
  "summary": "A khan at Karakorum could not govern China, Iran, and the Volga by riding to each of them, so he ruled through a chain of horses instead: a tablet at one station bought a fresh mount, a guide, and a bed at the next. It was never one road or one budget, and no single decree founded it and no single decree ended it — a Chinese registry, a Persian reform, and a steppe household levy all administered pieces of the same idea under different names, and it fractured along with the khanates after 1259 into the separate posts of Yuan China, Ilkhanid Iran, and the Golden Horde.",
